#!/bin/sh
#
# rasputin-init — the PID 1 shim that hands systemd a PERSISTENT machine-id and
# a PERSISTENT clock floor.
#
# It restores two things, for the same reason and out of the same read: both
# have to be in place BEFORE systemd starts, and the persistent partition is the
# only place either value can come from. The machine-id half is
# geekdojo-brain#600; the clock half is geekdojo-brain#601's arm64 side, and
# there is a section on it below the machine-id reasoning.
#
# post-build.sh points /sbin/init at this script, so the KERNEL runs it as PID 1
# and it execs the real systemd as its last act. Nothing else about the boot
# changes.
#
# ── why PID 1 and not a unit ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# /etc is a read-only squashfs, so systemd cannot store a machine-id. It mints a
# random one into /run/machine-id and bind-mounts it over /etc/machine-id, and it
# does that in machine_id_setup() — BEFORE the manager exists, so before
# generators run and long before the first unit. Measured on cp-compute2 across
# three reboots: e0d5a650… → f1d7a2b2… → b98cc8fe… (geekdojo-brain#600).
#
# That ordering is the whole problem. Anything that runs as a unit — a generator,
# a .mount ordered into sysinit.target, firstboot — is LATE: PID 1 has already
# cached the transient id and journald has already opened
# /run/log/journal/<transient>/system.journal and stamped _MACHINE_ID on every
# entry so far. Replacing /etc/machine-id underneath them leaves PID 1 and
# journald on the old value while later readers see the new one, which is worse
# than today's at-least-consistent-per-boot behaviour. So the value has to be in
# place before systemd starts, and on this image there are exactly two places
# earlier than systemd: an initrd, and /sbin/init.
#
#   - initrd: there is none (`/proc/cmdline`: root=PARTUUID=… rootfstype=squashfs
#     ro rootwait — no initrd=), and introducing one means changing the boot
#     media, which RAUC does not update (post-image.sh puts only rootfs.img in
#     the bundle) — so it would reach a node only at its next REFLASH.
#   - systemd.machine_id= on the kernel command line: same problem, and worse,
#     because the value is per-node, so the boot FAT / grub.cfg would have to be
#     rewritten on a running node — bootloader surgery with no fallback on the Pi.
#   - /sbin/init: lives in the rootfs, which is exactly what RAUC updates, and is
#     covered by the existing A/B rollback if it ever breaks.
#
# systemd's own answer for a read-only /etc is systemd-machine-id-commit.service,
# which commits the transient id once /etc becomes writable. Ours never does —
# the unit is skipped on every boot with "ConditionPathIsReadWrite=/etc was not
# met" (bench.local, 2026-09-22). This script plus
# rasputin-machine-id-commit.service are the same idea with the persistent
# partition standing in for a writable /etc.
#
# ── what it does ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Mount /proc and /sys (the kernel mounts neither before init, and findfs needs
# sysfs to find a filesystem by LABEL with udev not yet running), mount the
# persistent partition read-only, read /var/lib/rasputin/machine-id and
# /var/lib/rasputin/clock, unmount all three, and then:
#
#   - if and only if the id is a well-formed machine-id — write it to
#     /run/machine-id and bind-mount that over /etc/machine-id. That is byte for
#     byte the end state systemd produces today (same path, same 0444, same
#     bind); only the VALUE is different, and systemd's machine_id_setup() then
#     finds a valid /etc/machine-id and mints nothing;
#   - if and only if the stored clock is a plausible Unix timestamp AND is LATER
#     than what the kernel's clock currently reads — set the clock to it.
#
# ONE mount serves both. Reading a second file off a partition that is already
# mounted costs nothing, and doing it anywhere else would mean mounting it
# twice or waiting for the real mount, which is far too late (see the clock
# section above).
#
# It never creates, formats or writes the persistent partition. The id is put
# there by rasputin-machine-id-commit.service and the timestamp by
# rasputin-clock-save.service, both ordinary units that run once the partition
# is properly mounted. So the FIRST boot of a node (and the first boot after
# this lands on a fielded node) still uses a transient id and the image's baked
# /usr/lib/clock-epoch — both are then written to the store and kept.
#
# ── the clock floor, and why it is here too ───────────────────────────────────
#
# A node with no battery RTC — the Pi SKU — boots with no clock at all. PID 1
# advances time to a floor and systemd-timesyncd corrects it several seconds
# later. Measured on cp-compute5.local, wall clock against monotonic:
#
#   systemd[1]                      [ 5.861] "System time before build time,
#                                             advancing clock."  -> 2025-06-25
#   systemd-journald.service        2025-06-25 17:17:44 UTC   mono  8.588
#   rasputin-journal-store.service  2025-06-25 17:17:47 UTC   mono 11.522
#   systemd-journal-flush.service   2025-06-25 17:17:48 UTC   mono 12.004
#   systemd-timesyncd.service       2026-09-22 21:14:18 UTC   mono 12.868
#
# journald therefore flushes /run into the persistent store 0.8 s BEFORE the
# clock is corrected, stamping that boot's entries fifteen months in the past,
# and force-rotates the moment time moves — "Realtime clock jumped backwards
# relative to last journal entry, rotating." The Pi loses its previous boot's
# journal on every boot while the amd64 node, which has an RTC, keeps it. So
# persistent journals (#601) work on one SKU and not the other.
#
# WHY THE FLOOR HAS TO BE HERE AND NOT IN A UNIT. Everything above happens
# before the first unit that could help. rasputin-clock-floor.service runs at
# mono ~12, i.e. after journald has already written four seconds of entries that
# the flush will copy; it cannot fix anything earlier than itself, and neither
# can rasputin-journal-store.service, which is ordered before the flush but well
# after journald started. The only hook earlier than journald is the one earlier
# than systemd, and that is this script — which is already mounting the
# persistent partition read-only and already reading a file off it.
#
# WHAT IT READS: /var/lib/rasputin/clock, a decimal Unix timestamp written at
# shutdown and hourly by rasputin-clock-save.service. This is the fake-hwclock
# pattern every no-RTC distro ships, moved one stage earlier than a unit can
# reach.
#
# IT IS A FLOOR, NOT A CLOCK. The stored value is applied ONLY when the current
# time is earlier than it. So on the n100, whose RTC has already given the
# kernel a correct time, this is a no-op by construction; on the Pi it replaces
# a 15-month-stale guess with the node's own last-known-good time. It does not
# make an offline node's time correct and it replaces nothing about NTP.
#
# IT IS NOT THE ONLY FLOOR, AND THE THREE DO NOT COMPETE:
#
#   1. this, from /var/lib/rasputin/clock — the newest source, and the only one
#      early enough to be in front of journald;
#   2. /usr/lib/clock-epoch, stamped with the IMAGE build date by
#      post-fakeroot.sh — PID 1's own backstop, read by systemd's
#      clock_apply_epoch() a moment after we hand off. It applies when this
#      script found no store: a node's first boot ever, or one whose persistent
#      partition could not be read;
#   3. the timesyncd timestamp file, seeded by rasputin-clock-floor.service —
#      no longer a floor at all. It exists so timesyncd has a WRITABLE state
#      directory on a read-only rootfs, and it is stamped from whatever the
#      clock already reads, so it can never pull time backwards.
#
# Each one is strictly older than the one above it, so whichever applies, the
# result is a monotone floor and never a fight.
#
# ── the one rule ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# THIS SCRIPT MUST ALWAYS REACH THE HAND-OFF. It is PID 1: an exit, a hang or a
# non-zero `set -e` trip is an unbootable node, and the kernel does not fall back
# (it panics with "Requested init … failed"). So there is no `set -e`, no
# `set -u`, every command is guarded, and every path through the script ends at
# hand_off. A node whose persistent partition is missing, unformatted, wedged or
# empty boots exactly as it does today, with a transient id. test/machine-id-test.sh
# pins that, including the case where every helper binary is absent.
#
# Overrides are read ONLY when this is not PID 1 and RASPUTIN_INIT_TEST is set,
# so the real boot path takes nothing from the environment. (The kernel passes
# unrecognised cmdline key=value pairs to init as environment variables, and
# anyone who can write the kernel command line can already pass init=/bin/sh —
# but there is no reason to widen that.)

SYSTEMD=/usr/lib/systemd/systemd

hand_off() {
	exec "$SYSTEMD" "$@"
	# Unreachable unless exec itself failed (systemd deleted from the rootfs).
	# Nothing useful is left to try; let the kernel report it.
	exit 1
}

if [ "$$" -eq 1 ]; then
	# The real boot. Fixed paths; nothing is taken from the environment.
	ROOT=
	MOUNT=/bin/mount
	UMOUNT=/bin/umount
	FINDFS=/usr/sbin/findfs
	# busybox date. Both `-u +%s` and `-u -s @SECONDS` are busybox 1.37.0
	# applets on this image (`date --help` on cp-compute5.local lists
	# "@seconds_since_1970" among the recognised TIME formats). It is invoked
	# ONLY when a stored clock was read, so a node with no store never touches
	# it — see the clock block near the end.
	DATE=/bin/date
	KMSG=/dev/kmsg
elif [ -n "${RASPUTIN_INIT_TEST:-}" ]; then
	# test/machine-id-test.sh runs this script unprivileged against a scratch
	# tree with stub mount/umount/findfs and a stub hand-off target.
	ROOT=${RASPUTIN_INIT_ROOT:-}
	MOUNT=${RASPUTIN_INIT_MOUNT:-/bin/mount}
	UMOUNT=${RASPUTIN_INIT_UMOUNT:-/bin/umount}
	FINDFS=${RASPUTIN_INIT_FINDFS:-/usr/sbin/findfs}
	# test/clock-floor-test.sh ALWAYS overrides this with a stub that records
	# the call instead of making it: a suite that let the real `date -s` through
	# would set the clock of whatever machine ran it the moment it ran as root.
	DATE=${RASPUTIN_INIT_DATE:-/bin/date}
	KMSG=${RASPUTIN_INIT_KMSG:-/dev/null}
	SYSTEMD=${RASPUTIN_INIT_SYSTEMD:-$SYSTEMD}
else
	# Somebody ran /sbin/init by hand. Behave exactly as the symlink used to.
	hand_off "$@"
fi

PERSIST_LABEL=persistent
PERSIST_MNT=$ROOT/var/lib/rasputin
# The last-known-good time, written by rasputin-clock-save.service. Named
# "clock" to match the file systemd-timesyncd keeps for the same purpose; the
# two are read by different things at different stages and never share a path.
CLOCK_STORE_NAME=clock
RUN=$ROOT/run
RUN_MACHINE_ID=$RUN/machine-id
ETC_MACHINE_ID=$ROOT/etc/machine-id
PROC=$ROOT/proc
SYS=$ROOT/sys

# Log to /dev/kmsg: journald imports the kernel ring buffer, so these lines end
# up in the journal even though nothing is running yet to receive them. stderr
# is the console at this point, which is where a failed boot is read from.
log() {
	echo "rasputin-init: $*" >&2
	echo "rasputin-init: $*" >"$KMSG" 2>/dev/null
}

# machine_id_valid VALUE — systemd's own rule: exactly 32 lowercase hex digits,
# not all zeros. Anything else is refused rather than passed on, because a
# malformed /etc/machine-id would make systemd mint a transient one anyway and
# we would have spent a mount to arrive back where we started.
machine_id_valid() {
	[ "${#1}" -eq 32 ] || return 1
	case "$1" in
		*[!0123456789abcdef]*) return 1 ;;
		00000000000000000000000000000000) return 1 ;;
	esac
	return 0
}

# clock_valid VALUE — a plausible stored Unix timestamp: EXACTLY ten decimal
# digits, which is 2001-09-09 through 2286-11-20.
#
# Ten and not "ten or more". The value goes into `[ "$a" -ge "$b" ]`, and this
# runs under busybox ash, whose test applet is not a place to find out how wide
# an integer is; ten digits cannot overflow anything and eleven might. The cost
# is that this stops working in 2286, which is stated rather than discovered:
# the node then degrades to /usr/lib/clock-epoch, exactly as one with no store.
#
# Nothing narrower is needed at the low end. The value is only ever applied when
# it is LATER than the current clock, so a stale-but-well-formed one is a no-op
# rather than a wrong answer, and a corrupt one that is not ten digits is
# refused here before `date` ever sees it.
clock_valid() {
	[ "${#1}" -eq 10 ] || return 1
	case "$1" in
		*[!0123456789]*) return 1 ;;
	esac
	return 0
}

# is_mounted PATH — true when PATH is a mount point. Reads /proc/self/mounts
# directly: `mountpoint` is not on this image and the kernel has not mounted
# /proc for us.
is_mounted() {
	[ -r "$PROC/self/mounts" ] || return 1
	while read -r _im_src _im_dst _im_rest; do
		[ "$_im_dst" = "$1" ] && return 0
	done <"$PROC/self/mounts"
	return 1
}

# unmount_persist — put the persistent partition back, and SAY SO if it will
# not go. Leaving it mounted is not harmless: systemd derives .mount state from
# /proc/self/mountinfo, so var-lib-rasputin.mount would come up "mounted"
# without ever running mount(8). x-systemd.makefs and x-systemd.growfs would
# never run — rasputin-growpart's expansion silently stops happening — and
# ConditionPathIsMountPoint=/var/lib/rasputin would pass, so the commit unit
# runs rather than skipping and then fails writing to a read-only filesystem —
# as does anything else that writes under /var/lib/rasputin this boot. Every
# one of those is silent, which is why the failure gets a line of its own.
unmount_persist() {
	"$UMOUNT" "$PERSIST_MNT" 2>/dev/null && return 0
	log "could not unmount $PERSIST_MNT; it stays READ-ONLY for this whole boot, systemd will not mount it itself, and the commit will fail"
	return 0
}

# ── the API filesystems ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The kernel mounts NEITHER /proc nor /sys before it runs init; systemd's
# mount_setup() does that, and it runs after we have handed off. /dev is the one
# exception — the kernel mounts devtmpfs itself (CONFIG_DEVTMPFS_MOUNT=y,
# confirmed in /proc/config.gz on the rpi nodes; the n100 has no /proc/config.gz,
# but this script's own log line reaches /dev/kmsg on BOTH SKUs, which is the
# proof that matters).
#
# Both are mounted with systemd's own mount_setup() options, so that a failure
# to unmount one below leaves an end state byte for byte what a normal boot
# produces — systemd skips an API filesystem that is already a mount point.
#
# Neither mount is allowed to stop the hand-off: a failure is logged and the
# script carries on to exec systemd with a transient id, exactly as it would on
# a node with no persistent partition at all.

# /proc — to see what is already mounted (is_mounted below reads
# /proc/self/mounts; `mountpoint` is not on this image).
proc_is_ours=
if [ ! -r "$PROC/self/mounts" ]; then
	if "$MOUNT" -t proc -o nosuid,nodev,noexec proc "$PROC" 2>/dev/null; then
		proc_is_ours=yes
	else
		log "could not mount $PROC"
	fi
fi

# /sys — this is what dev.251 was missing (geekdojo-brain#600). udev is not
# running, so /dev/disk/by-label does not exist and libblkid cannot resolve a
# LABEL= from it. Its fallback is a full device scan, and that scan is driven
# from SYSFS, not from /proc/partitions: strace of util-linux 2.40.4 findfs —
# the node's exact version — shows it opendir()s /sys/block and then reads
# /sys/dev/block/<maj>:<min> for each entry. With /sys unmounted the first
# openat() returns ENOENT, the scan yields nothing, and findfs exits 1 printing
# nothing. That is precisely the empty $persist_dev observed on both SKUs.
#
# Tested in a container with udev absent and the libblkid cache removed (so a
# cache hit cannot mask the dependency), on util-linux 2.38.1 and 2.40.4:
# /sys mounted -> findfs resolves the label; /sys unmounted -> "unable to
# resolve". `blkid` on the device node directly still reads the label without
# /sys, so it is the ENUMERATION that needs sysfs, not the superblock read.
#
# Probed with /sys/block rather than is_mounted(), so this works even when the
# /proc mount above failed. /sys/block exists in every mounted sysfs and cannot
# exist in the baked, empty /sys of a read-only squashfs.
sysfs_is_ours=
if [ ! -d "$SYS/block" ]; then
	if "$MOUNT" -t sysfs -o nosuid,nodev,noexec sysfs "$SYS" 2>/dev/null; then
		sysfs_is_ours=yes
		log "mounted $SYS for the block-device scan"
	else
		log "could not mount $SYS; findfs cannot enumerate block devices without it"
	fi
fi

# ── read the stored id ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# By filesystem LABEL, which post-image.sh's `mke2fs -L persistent` gives both
# SKUs — so one lookup serves the n100's GPT and the rpi's MBR, whose fstabs
# address the same partition differently (PARTLABEL vs PARTUUID). Mounted over
# its own final mountpoint, which is baked and empty, so no scratch directory is
# needed on a read-only rootfs; systemd's fstab unit mounts it properly (rw,
# x-systemd.growfs) later in local-fs.target.
#
# The key is no looser than the one the image already trusts: the rpi's fstab
# uses PARTUUID=52415350-07, and genimage pins disk-signature = 0x52415350 so
# that PARTUUID is identical on EVERY flashed medium; the n100's PARTLABEL is
# likewise the genimage partition name, identical on every medium. So a node
# booted with a second Rasputin disk attached is ambiguous for systemd's own
# /var/lib/rasputin mount exactly as it is here — and the persistent filesystem
# baked into the image is empty, so the worst this lookup does with the wrong
# one is read no id and degrade. Every message about the LOOKUP names the
# device that was chosen, and so does the restore line, so an ambiguous pick
# shows up in the boot log rather than as a mystery.
#
# store_why records WHICH step failed. dev.251 emitted one message — "no stored
# machine-id yet" — for three different conditions (findfs found nothing, the
# mount failed, the file was absent or unreadable), which is why the sysfs
# defect had to be diagnosed by elimination instead of by reading a boot log
# (geekdojo-brain#600). Exactly one of stored_id / store_why ends up set, and
# the single fallback line below quotes store_why verbatim. A silent degrade is
# the failure mode this component is most prone to, so every branch says its
# piece, and names $persist_dev whenever findfs gave us one.
#
# The clock is read in the SAME mount window and reported the same way, with
# its own why-line, because its failure modes are its own: a node can perfectly
# well have a committed machine-id and no stored clock (the first boot after
# this change lands on a fielded node) or the other way round.
stored_id=
store_why=
stored_clock=
clock_why=
persist_dev=$("$FINDFS" "LABEL=$PERSIST_LABEL" 2>/dev/null)
if [ -z "$persist_dev" ]; then
	store_why="findfs found no filesystem labelled $PERSIST_LABEL"
	clock_why="$store_why"
elif ! "$MOUNT" -t ext4 -o ro,nosuid,nodev,noexec "$persist_dev" "$PERSIST_MNT" 2>/dev/null; then
	store_why="found $persist_dev but could not mount it read-only on $PERSIST_MNT"
	clock_why="$store_why"
else
	if [ ! -r "$PERSIST_MNT/machine-id" ]; then
		# The EXPECTED case on a node's first boot and on the first boot after
		# this image lands on a fielded one: the commit unit has not written the
		# store yet. Worded so it does not read like a fault, because it is not
		# one. Guarded by -r because a redirection onto a missing file is an
		# error the shell reports, not a value.
		store_why="no machine-id stored on $persist_dev yet"
	else
		# read -r, not cat: the file is one short line and this needs no fork.
		read -r stored_id <"$PERSIST_MNT/machine-id" 2>/dev/null
		if [ -z "$stored_id" ]; then
			store_why="mounted $persist_dev but its machine-id is empty"
		elif ! machine_id_valid "$stored_id"; then
			# Refused rather than passed on: systemd would mint a transient id
			# anyway, so binding garbage buys nothing and hides the fault.
			store_why="mounted $persist_dev but its machine-id is malformed: $stored_id"
			stored_id=
		fi
	fi

	if [ ! -r "$PERSIST_MNT/$CLOCK_STORE_NAME" ]; then
		# Expected on a first boot and on the first boot after this change
		# reaches a fielded node; systemd's own /usr/lib/clock-epoch covers it.
		clock_why="no clock stored on $persist_dev yet"
	else
		read -r stored_clock <"$PERSIST_MNT/$CLOCK_STORE_NAME" 2>/dev/null
		if [ -z "$stored_clock" ]; then
			clock_why="mounted $persist_dev but its clock is empty"
		elif ! clock_valid "$stored_clock"; then
			clock_why="mounted $persist_dev but its clock is malformed: $stored_clock"
			stored_clock=
		fi
	fi

	# ONE unmount for both reads. Everything above this line only reads; the
	# partition goes back before anything is delivered, so a delivery that goes
	# wrong cannot leave it mounted.
	unmount_persist
fi

# ── deliver it ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
if [ -n "$stored_id" ]; then
	# /run is where systemd itself puts the machine-id, so put it there with
	# systemd's exact mount_setup() options: it skips a /run that is already a
	# mount point, and matching the options means it cannot tell the difference.
	# Do not "simplify" these — a plain `mount -t tmpfs tmpfs /run` would ship a
	# /run with no size limit, no nr_inodes limit and nosuid/nodev off.
	if is_mounted "$RUN" || "$MOUNT" -t tmpfs \
		-o mode=0755,nosuid,nodev,strictatime,size=20%,nr_inodes=800k \
		tmpfs "$RUN" 2>/dev/null
	then
		# 0444 via umask rather than a chmod: same mode systemd gives its own
		# /run/machine-id, and one fewer binary this has to find as PID 1.
		if (umask 0222; printf '%s\n' "$stored_id" >"$RUN_MACHINE_ID") 2>/dev/null; then
			# -o bind is understood by both busybox and util-linux mount
			# (the idiom rasputin-coredump-store.service already uses).
			if "$MOUNT" -o bind "$RUN_MACHINE_ID" "$ETC_MACHINE_ID" 2>/dev/null; then
				log "machine-id $stored_id restored from $persist_dev (LABEL=$PERSIST_LABEL)"
			else
				# systemd will overwrite /run/machine-id with a transient value
				# and bind that instead, which is exactly today's behaviour.
				log "read $stored_id from $persist_dev but could not bind /run/machine-id over /etc/machine-id; falling back to a transient id"
			fi
		else
			log "read $stored_id from $persist_dev but could not write /run/machine-id; falling back to a transient id"
		fi
	else
		log "read $stored_id from $persist_dev but could not mount /run; falling back to a transient id"
	fi
else
	# store_why is set on every branch above, so the default is a backstop, not
	# a case: it is here so that a branch added later without a message still
	# produces a line rather than degrading in silence, which is the whole
	# defect this half of the change exists to end. The benign reason — the
	# commit unit has not written the store yet — is the "no machine-id stored
	# on … yet" one; every other reason is a fault and names its step.
	log "${store_why:-no stored machine-id yet}; this boot uses a transient id"
fi

# ── deliver the clock floor ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ONLY FORWARDS. The stored value is applied when, and only when, the kernel's
# clock currently reads earlier than it. On the n100, whose RTC has already put
# the kernel at the right time, every boot takes the "already ahead" branch and
# nothing is set — the whole block is a no-op on that SKU by construction.
#
# $DATE is not touched at all unless a stored clock was read, so a node with no
# store, an unreadable partition or no date binary behaves exactly as it does
# today, and so does any harness that does not stub $DATE.
if [ -n "$stored_clock" ]; then
	now=$("$DATE" -u +%s 2>/dev/null)
	if ! clock_valid "$now"; then
		# A date binary that is missing, or that answered something this cannot
		# compare, is not a reason to guess: leaving the clock alone puts the
		# boot back to systemd's own /usr/lib/clock-epoch, which is the backstop
		# this is layered on top of.
		log "read clock $stored_clock from $persist_dev but $DATE would not report the current time ('${now:-<nothing>}'); leaving the clock alone"
	elif [ "$now" -ge "$stored_clock" ]; then
		# The n100's every boot, and any Pi that somehow already has the time.
		log "clock floor: already at $now, at or ahead of the $stored_clock stored on $persist_dev; leaving it alone"
	elif "$DATE" -u -s "@$stored_clock" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
		log "clock advanced from $now to $stored_clock, restored from $persist_dev (LABEL=$PERSIST_LABEL)"
	else
		# systemd's clock_apply_epoch() still runs a moment from now and will
		# apply /usr/lib/clock-epoch, so this degrades to the image build date
		# rather than to 1970.
		log "read clock $stored_clock from $persist_dev but $DATE could not set the clock; falling back to the image's baked /usr/lib/clock-epoch"
	fi
else
	# Same backstop shape as the machine-id line above: every branch that leaves
	# $stored_clock empty sets $clock_why, and the default is here so a branch
	# added later without a message still produces a line.
	log "${clock_why:-no stored clock yet}; this boot's floor is the image's baked /usr/lib/clock-epoch"
fi

# Leave nothing behind that systemd would not have made itself. Unmounted in
# the reverse of the order they were mounted; a failure here is harmless,
# because what stays behind is what systemd's mount_setup() would have made.
[ -n "$sysfs_is_ours" ] && "$UMOUNT" "$SYS" 2>/dev/null
[ -n "$proc_is_ours" ] && "$UMOUNT" "$PROC" 2>/dev/null

hand_off "$@"
